THERMAL-CRACK EFFECTIVE-MEDIUM CALCULATIONS

This distribution contains the C++ and MATLAB software accompanying

  Korenaga, Kawakatsu, and Takeuchi,
  Effects of thermal cracks on the seismic anisotropy of oceanic
  lithosphere, JGR: Solid Earth manuscript 2026JB034946.

The C++ software evaluates Hill's polarization tensor for an arbitrarily
oriented ellipsoidal inclusion in a transversely isotropic elastic host.  It
also calculates dilute dry and fluid-saturated effective stiffness tensors.
The MATLAB software generates the numerical tables used for the main figures
and reproduces the PREM1 and PREM2 figures.


DIRECTORY CONTENTS

  include/emt              C++ header files
  src                      C++ implementation files
  apps                     C++ command-line programs
  tests                    C++ unit tests
  scripts                  C++ build, demonstration, and reproduction scripts
  kanaun2009_fourier       Kanaun and Levin (2009) Fourier implementation
  matlab/matlab            MATLAB functions
  matlab/data              Effective tensors, PREM files, and MINEOS output
  matlab/results           Precomputed numerical tables
  matlab/figures           PREM1 and PREM2 in EPS and PS formats
  CMakeLists.txt           CMake build definition
  Makefile                 Make build definition
  CITATION.cff             Machine-readable citation information
  LICENSE                  BSD 3-Clause License

Compiled files and temporary build directories are not included.


C++ REQUIREMENTS

The C++ software requires a C++17 compiler.  CMake 3.16 or newer is
recommended.  The included Makefile provides an alternative for systems
without CMake.


BUILDING AND TESTING THE C++ SOFTWARE

Run the following command from the top-level directory:

  ./scripts/build.sh

The script uses CMake when it is available and otherwise uses Make.  It builds
the four command-line programs and runs the unit and command-line tests.

The corresponding manual CMake commands are:

  cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
  cmake --build build --parallel
  ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure

The corresponding Make commands are:

  make
  make test

To remove files generated by Make, run:

  make clean


QUICK C++ DEMONSTRATION

After building the software, run:

  ./scripts/quick_demo.sh

This performs short calculations with all four programs.  The CSV output is
written under results/quick_demo.


C++ COMMAND-LINE PROGRAMS

All long options require a space between the option name and its value.
Stiffness and fluid bulk modulus must use the same units; the examples below
use GPa.

1. Withers analytical benchmark

  ./build/emt_withers_benchmark --aspect 0.01 --ntheta 400 --weight 0.4

Available options are:

  --aspect VALUE       Set a3/a1.  The default is 0.01.
  --ntheta N           Set the initial polar divisions.  The default is 100.
  --weight VALUE       Set the adaptive weight.  The default is 0.4.
  --max-level N        Set the recursion limit.  The default is 20.
  --stiffness CSV      Set C11,C12,C13,C33,C44.
  --help               Print the program usage.

The default stiffness is 47,8,5,34,17 GPa.  Output is CSV written to standard
output.

2. Guo et al. (2019) comparison

  ./build/emt_guo_comparison --aspect 0.01 --ntheta 800 \
    --angle-step 1 > guo.csv

Available options are:

  --aspect VALUE       Set a3/a1.  The default is 0.01.
  --ntheta N           Set the initial polar divisions.  The default is 200.
  --weight VALUE       Set the adaptive weight.  The default is 0.4.
  --max-level N        Set the recursion limit.  The default is 20.
  --angle-step DEG     Set the inclination increment.  The default is 10.
  --stiffness CSV      Set C11,C12,C13,C33,C44.
  --help               Print the program usage.

The default stiffness is 47,8,5,34,17 GPa.  The implementation of the
published comparison formula includes the factor-of-four correction discussed
in the manuscript.  Output is CSV written to standard output.

3. Random-orientation calculation

  ./build/emt_random_orientations --samples 10000 --seed 20260813 \
    --aspect 0.01 --ntheta 400 --output-every 10 > random.csv

Available options are:

  --samples N          Set the number of orientations.  The default is 100.
  --seed N             Set the random seed.  The default is 20260813.
  --output-every N     Set the running-average interval.  The default is 10.
  --aspect VALUE       Set a3/a1.  The default is 0.01.
  --ntheta N           Set the initial polar divisions.  The default is 100.
  --weight VALUE       Set the adaptive weight.  The default is 0.4.
  --max-level N        Set the recursion limit.  The default is 20.
  --stiffness CSV      Set C11,C12,C13,C33,C44.
  --help               Print the program usage.

The default stiffness is 47,8,5,34,17 GPa.  Output includes adaptive running
averages and the corrected Xu et al. (2020) comparison tensor.  It is written
as CSV to standard output.  The seeded calculation is deterministic.

4. Effective-stiffness calculation

  ./build/emt_effective_stiffness --configuration horizontal \
    --aspect 0.01 --porosity 0.00209 --fluid-bulk 2.9 \
    --dip 45 --ntheta 1600

Available options are:

  --configuration NAME   Select single, hexagonal, or horizontal.
                         The default is single.
  --aspect VALUE         Set the minor/major semi-axis ratio.
                         The default is 0.01.
  --porosity VALUE       Set the inclusion volume fraction.
                         The default is 0.001.
  --azimuth DEG          Set the reference azimuth for single or hexagonal
                         cracks.  The default is 0 degrees.
  --dip DEG              Rotate a horizontal crack about y.
                         The default is 0 degrees.
  --fluid-bulk VALUE     Set the fluid bulk modulus.
                         The default is 2.2 GPa.
  --dry                  Skip Brown-Korringa fluid substitution.
  --ntheta N             Set the initial polar divisions.
                         The default is 200.
  --weight VALUE         Set the adaptive weight.  The default is 0.4.
  --max-level N          Set the recursion limit.  The default is 20.
  --stiffness CSV        Set C11,C12,C13,C33,C44.
  --help                 Print the program usage.

The default stiffness is 57.624,19.88,14.2,41.16,13.48 GPa.  Output is CSV
written to standard output and includes all 36 effective-stiffness components
in row-major Voigt order.


REPRODUCING THE C++ APPENDIX CALCULATIONS

Build the software first.  The following scripts write CSV files under the
top-level results directory:

  ./scripts/reproduce_appendix_a2.sh
  ./scripts/reproduce_appendix_a3.sh
  ./scripts/reproduce_appendix_a4.sh
  ./scripts/reproduce_appendix_a5.sh

The default settings reproduce the manuscript calculations and can be
computationally expensive.  Settings may be changed for shorter checks by
placing variable assignments before a command.

The A2 script accepts NTHETA_VALUES, WEIGHT_VALUES, and ASPECT_VALUES.

  NTHETA_VALUES="100 200" ASPECT_VALUES="0.1 0.01" \
    ./scripts/reproduce_appendix_a2.sh

The A3 script accepts NTHETA and ANGLE_STEP.

  NTHETA=200 ANGLE_STEP=10 ./scripts/reproduce_appendix_a3.sh

The A4 script accepts SAMPLES, NTHETA, SEED, and OUTPUT_EVERY.

  SAMPLES=100 NTHETA=100 OUTPUT_EVERY=10 \
    ./scripts/reproduce_appendix_a4.sh

The A5 script accepts NTHETA, ASPECT_VALUES, and DIP_VALUES.

  NTHETA=200 ASPECT_VALUES="0.1 0.01" DIP_VALUES="0 30 60 90" \
    ./scripts/reproduce_appendix_a5.sh


C++ CONVENTIONS AND SCOPE

Voigt order is 11,22,33,23,13,12.  Euler angles are intrinsic
yaw-pitch-roll rotations about z-y'-x'', in degrees.  For an axisymmetric
oblate inclusion, the shortest axis is the crack normal.  Aspect ratio is the
minor semi-axis divided by a major semi-axis in the axisymmetric examples.

The single configuration is a vertical crack whose shortest axis initially
lies along y.  The hexagonal configuration contains three such cracks separated
by 120 degrees in azimuth.  The horizontal configuration has its shortest axis
initially along z and is rotated about y by the specified dip.

The effective-medium calculation uses the dilute, non-interacting-inclusion
approximation.  Fluid substitution follows Brown and Korringa (1975).  The
Green-function expression is specific to isotropic and transversely isotropic
hosts and is not a general-anisotropy solver.


KANAUN AND LEVIN (2009) FOURIER CALCULATION

The kanaun2009_fourier directory contains an independent implementation of the
constant-stress, zero-thickness elliptical-crack method in equations (23)-(32)
of Kanaun and Levin (2009).  It depends on the main C++ library in the parent
directory.

Build it from the top-level directory with:

  make -C kanaun2009_fourier

Run the complete benchmark with:

  ./kanaun2009_fourier/run_benchmark.sh

The output is written to kanaun2009_fourier/results/benchmark.csv.  The run
checks the numerical integral against equations (76)-(77) and (82), evaluates
the setup of Figure 4, and compares the zero-thickness result with the limiting
finite-oblate-spheroid calculation.

The benchmark executable can also be run directly:

  ./kanaun2009_fourier/build/benchmark_kanaun09 \
    -P360 -Q64 -N800 -W0.4 > benchmark.csv

Its options are appended directly to each option letter:

  -C<c11/c12/c13/c33/c44>   Set the TI stiffness components.
  -P<nphi>                   Set the azimuthal quadrature count.
  -Q<nq>                     Set the transformed q3 quadrature count.
  -N<ntheta>                 Set the finite-spheroid polar divisions.
  -W<factor>                 Set the finite-spheroid adaptive weight.
  -F                         Skip the finite-aspect-ratio comparison.

The default TI stiffness is 47/8/5/34/17 GPa.  In the limiting comparison,
aspect_ratio times H is compared with the zero-thickness crack compliance,
because H itself diverges as aspect ratio approaches zero at fixed porosity.
The Kanaun method represents an ideal zero-thickness crack; finite aspect ratios
are handled by the main C++ implementation.

To build the Fourier directory when it is not located immediately below the
main package, provide the main package path to Make:

  make -C kanaun2009_fourier EMT_ROOT=/path/to/main/package

To remove its generated build directory, run:

  make -C kanaun2009_fourier clean

Reference:

  S. K. Kanaun and V. M. Levin (2009), Elliptical cracks arbitrarily
  oriented in 3D-anisotropic elastic media, International Journal of
  Engineering Science, 47, 777-792,
  doi:10.1016/j.ijengsci.2008.12.014.


MATLAB REQUIREMENTS

The MATLAB package was tested with MATLAB R2024b and does not require any
MATLAB toolboxes.  All paths are determined from the package location.  No
source file needs to be edited after moving the distribution.


RUNNING THE MATLAB SOFTWARE

Start MATLAB and change to the matlab/matlab directory in this distribution.
The principal commands are:

  self_test
  run_all
  validate_release
  plot_PREM_figures

self_test performs compact checks of numerical integration and conversion from
elastic tensors to velocities.

run_all regenerates the effective-velocity, thermal-crack scaling, SKS, and
surface-wave tables.  It then runs validate_release.  Output is written under
matlab/results.  The surface-wave calculation reads all of the distributed
MINEOS eigenfunction files and can take appreciably longer than self_test.

validate_release checks the expected output dimensions, period ranges,
monotonic porosity values, positive velocities, and nonnegative SKS delay
times.  Precomputed output tables are included, so this command can be run
without first running run_all.

plot_PREM_figures reads the tables under matlab/results and writes PREM1.eps,
PREM1.ps, PREM2.eps, and PREM2.ps under matlab/figures.  Existing files with
these names are replaced.  Optional input and output directories may be
specified as follows:

  plot_PREM_figures('/path/to/results','/path/to/figures')

The individual data-generation stages may also be run from the matlab/matlab
directory:

  generate_velocity_tables
  generate_tcscale3_tables
  generate_sks_tables
  generate_surface_wave_tables

These functions write to locations returned by release_paths.  Normally,
run_all should be used because it creates output directories, runs all stages
in the required order, and validates the completed data set.


MATLAB DATA AND FIGURE CORRESPONDENCE

The following generated files provide the data for the main figures:

  CPO1 and CPO2
    matlab/results/common/vel_jungA.*.dat

  TCscale1 and TCscale2
    matlab/results/common/vel_jungA.*.dat
    matlab/results/common/vel_abram97.*.dat

  PREM1, aspect ratio 1e-2 and porosity 0.002
    matlab/results/surface_waves/dVR.m*.a1e-2.p0.002*.dat
    matlab/results/surface_waves/dVL.m*.a1e-2.p0.002*.dat

  PREM2, aspect ratio 1e-3 and porosity 0.001
    matlab/results/surface_waves/dVR.m*.a1e-3.p0.001*.dat
    matlab/results/surface_waves/dVL.m*.a1e-3.p0.001*.dat

  PREM3 and SKS
    matlab/results/common/SKS_dtmax.*.dat

  TCscale3
    matlab/results/common/TCscale3.*.dat

The PREM1 and PREM2 figures show four crack penetration depths.  Solid curves
show the hexagonal geometry and dashed curves show the single-orientation
geometry.


MATLAB NUMERICAL FILE FORMATS

All numerical tables are whitespace-delimited ASCII files.

Files named Csat_*.dat contain an identifier, crack porosity, and 36 stiffness
components in column-major 6 by 6 order.  Stiffness is in GPa.

The Cij.*.dat PREM files contain radius in m, density in kg/m^3, and 36
stiffness components in Pa.

Files named vel_*.dat contain the following 18 columns:

  porosity, VSV, VSH, xi, VPV, VPH, Bc, Bs, Gc, Gs, Hc, Hs,
  Ec, Es, A, L, F, N

Velocities are in m/s and elastic coefficients are in Pa.

Files named dVR.*.dat and dVL.*.dat contain spherical-harmonic degree, period
in s, isotropic-reference phase velocity in km/s, and the constant, cos(2 psi),
and cos(4 psi) phase-velocity perturbations in km/s.

Files named SKS_dtmax.*.dat contain crack porosity in percent and maximum delay
time in s.


USING OTHER MINEOS CALCULATIONS

To use a different PREM-compatible set of MINEOS output:

  1. Place spheroidal files in matlab/data/prem/eigen_rayleigh.
  2. Place toroidal files in matlab/data/prem/eigen_love.
  3. Replace the corresponding vel.prem_iso.2.dat,
     vel.prem_iso.3.dat, vel.prem_ocean.2.dat, and
     vel.prem_ocean.3.dat dispersion tables.
  4. Retain the eigenfunction naming convention
     S.0000000.0000000.ASC and T.0000000.0000000.ASC, with the final
     seven-digit field replaced by spherical-harmonic degree.
  5. Run run_all and then plot_PREM_figures.

The PREM stiffness grid and eigenfunction radius grids must be identical after
the eigenfunction arrays are reversed.  The distributed data include the modes
needed for the plotted period range from 10 to 250 s.  These files are numerical
output generated with MINEOS; the MINEOS source code is not distributed here.


